# PAPER_HORIZONS_MINUTES=30,60,120    <- DUPLICATE of line 225; set it THERE. Compose takes the LAST definition, so a value filled in above would be silently overridden by this one.

# A mark can only be taken on a tick, so a token that stops pricing gets its
# mark late. Marks later than this multiple of their horizon are still
# recorded but excluded from the summary: a price fetched 50 minutes late
# does not describe a 30-minute return.
# PAPER_HORIZON_TOLERANCE=1.5    <- DUPLICATE of line 231; set it THERE. Compose takes the LAST definition, so a value filled in above would be silently overridden by this one.

# --- Stage 2: short-window features (recorded, never gated on) -----------
#
# m5 is the finest bucket DexScreener publishes -- there is nothing below
# five minutes. That is not a limitation worth working around: a token doing
# 50 trades an hour has no trades at all in most 10-second windows, so a
# shorter window is mostly measuring emptiness. A signal is also a
# commitment to react on its timescale, and between the tick loop,
# DexScreener's caching, Jupiter quoting and ~400ms blocks, anything
# sub-minute belongs to bots we would be trading against, not alongside.
#
# The m5 fields cost nothing extra -- they arrive in the same response the
# price comes from -- and they exist so paper_trading.feature_correlations()
# can test whether they predict the horizon returns BEFORE any gate depends
# on them. There is no setting here to turn them on; they are always
# recorded and never acted upon.

# Minimum observations before a correlation is worth reading. Below this,
# |rho| of 0.3 turns up by chance often enough to mislead.
# (Set in paper_trading.MIN_CORRELATION_N; listed here for visibility.)

# --- Display timezone -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Which zone the dashboard clock and log lines are rendered in. Any IANA zone
# name. Use a region name (America/New_York), not a fixed offset or
# abbreviation: America/New_York switches between EDT (UTC-4) and EST (UTC-5)
# on the correct dates by itself, whereas a fixed -5 would be an hour wrong
# for roughly eight months of the year.
#
# DISPLAY ONLY. Every timestamp column is TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE, so stored
# instants are absolute and changing this cannot reinterpret existing rows.
# Storing local wall-clock time instead would be actively unsafe here: Eastern
# repeats the 01:00-02:00 hour every November, so two different instants would
# share one timestamp -- and "which of these trades came first" is not a
# question a ledger should have to guess at.
#
# docker-compose.yml feeds this to TZ (the OS clock, for log lines) and PGTZ
# (so `docker compose exec db psql` prints the same zone the dashboard shows).
# APP_TIMEZONE=America/New_York    <- DUPLICATE of line 270; set it THERE. Compose takes the LAST definition, so a value filled in above would be silently overridden by this one.

# How long a paper trade may go WITHOUT A PRICE before it is abandoned.
#
# This closes a silent, one-directional bias. Both terminal checks in
# mark_to_market() sat downstream of "if the price is unknown, skip", so a
# trade whose token stopped pricing could never reach ANY end state -- it
# stayed OPEN forever, and results_summary() reads only CLOSED trades.
#
# The tokens that stop pricing are not a random sample. A delisted pair, a
# pulled pool, a rug -- those are the WORST outcomes in the population, and
# they were exactly the ones excluded from every average. Reported P&L was
# biased upward by an unknown amount, invisibly.
#
# Abandoned trades are recorded with status ABANDONED and NO fabricated exit
# price. They are excluded from the P&L averages and counted separately, so
# the dropout is visible rather than silent -- section 3b of stage2_check.sql.
# PAPER_UNPRICEABLE_ABANDON_MINUTES=180    <- DUPLICATE of line 287; set it THERE. Compose takes the LAST definition, so a value filled in above would be silently overridden by this one.

# --- Birdeye (read-only market data) --------------------------------------
#
# Replaces GeckoTerminal for token discovery. GeckoTerminal's free tier
# answered 429 from roughly the sixth call in a burst, which silently deleted
# whichever source was last in line -- see GECKOTERMINAL_MIN_INTERVAL_S below
# for the full story.
#
# Birdeye is a better fit for two concrete reasons, not just a higher limit:
#   - /defi/v3/token/list filters by min_liquidity SERVER-SIDE and returns up
#     to 100 rows per call, so a four-page walk plus client-side rejection of
#     ~90% of rows becomes one request that returns only what qualifies.
#   - /defi/v2/tokens/new_listing takes `time_to`, so recency paging walks
#     backwards through real timestamps instead of trusting that `page=N` is
#     honoured at all.
#
# Auth is an X-API-KEY header. Read-only: no signing key, no wallet, no
# custody -- same standing as every other provider here.
#
# 60 requests/minute is enforced PER SECOND, so there is no burst allowance.
# Budget per refresh is 4-5 calls; the throttle handles the spacing.
# BIRDEYE_API_KEY=    <- DUPLICATE of line 309; set it THERE. Compose takes the LAST definition, so a value filled in above would be silently overridden by this one.
# BIRDEYE_API_BASE=https://public-api.birdeye.so    <- DUPLICATE of line 310; set it THERE. Compose takes the LAST definition, so a value filled in above would be silently overridden by this one.

# --- Discovery breadth ----------------------------------------------------
#
# Added after Stage 2 plateaued: after ~18 hours the experiment had seen 50
# distinct tokens and stopped finding new ones entirely, with only 17 approved
# against the ~30 a verdict needs.
#
# The cause was not a bug. All three original sources -- RugCheck trending,
# DexScreener top boosts, GeckoTerminal trending pools -- are POPULARITY
# rankings, and popularity is sticky over hours by definition. Between them
# they returned the same ~34 tokens refresh after refresh.
#
# The fourth source walks the volume-ranked pool list instead, which
# enumerates the market rather than ranking the top of it. Page 4 contains
# tokens no trending list will ever surface.

# How many pages of the pool list to walk per refresh. GeckoTerminal's free
# tier allows 30 calls/minute; at one refresh per DISCOVERY_CACHE_TTL_S this
# costs 4 calls every three minutes. The walk stops early if a page adds no
# new tokens -- which covers both the end of the results and an endpoint that
# ignores `page` altogether, so a provider change cannot silently burn the
# rate limit re-reading page 1.
# DISCOVERY_POOL_PAGES=4    <- DUPLICATE of line 427; set it THERE. Compose takes the LAST definition, so a value filled in above would be silently overridden by this one.

# Raised from 60. The trending sources return ~34 unique between them, so 60
# was never binding and the new source would have been truncated away.
#
# A longer list does not slow the cycle: PAPER_REENTRY_COOLDOWN_MINUTES
# already limits how often one token can be recorded, so the effect is more
# DISTINCT tokens per hour rather than more repeats of the same ones.
#
# Raised again from 150 to leave room for the new-pool pen below. Truncation
# is first-source-wins, so a cap of 150 would have silently discarded every
# promotion while the logs still reported a healthy candidate count.
DISCOVERY_MAX_CANDIDATES=300

# Courtesy spacing between paginated calls within ONE source.
#
# This is not a rate-limit control and raising it does not fix a 429. That
# was tried at 1.2s and failed: the limit is per PROVIDER, and three of the
# five sources are GeckoTerminal, so spacing inside each source still let
# nine calls land in a ~12-second burst. GECKOTERMINAL_MIN_INTERVAL_S below
# is what actually governs the rate.
# DISCOVERY_PAGE_SPACING_S=0.4    <- DUPLICATE of line 431; set it THERE. Compose takes the LAST definition, so a value filled in above would be silently overridden by this one.

# Minimum gap between any two GeckoTerminal calls, across every source.
#
# One refresh spends up to nine calls there: 4 new-pool pages, trending, and
# 4 pool pages. Without this they arrived in a burst and GeckoTerminal
# answered 429 from the sixth call onward -- which silently deleted the pool
# walk (last in line) on every cycle and dropped the candidate list from ~101
# back to the ~32 the trending sources return on their own. Nothing crashed
# and no count looked obviously wrong.
#
# The documented allowance is 30/minute, but bursts are policed more tightly
# than that suggests. 2.5s caps us at 24/minute with no burst. Nine calls
# then take ~22s of a 180s refresh, costing one slow tick every three
# minutes -- cheap, because a 429 does not delay a source, it removes it.
# GECKOTERMINAL_MIN_INTERVAL_S=2.5    <- DUPLICATE of line 419; set it THERE. Compose takes the LAST definition, so a value filled in above would be silently overridden by this one.

# --- New-pool holding pen -------------------------------------------------
#
# Widening the pool source moved distinct tokens from 50 to 121 in one hour
# and then flattened again at ~1.5 new tokens/hour. That is the turnover rate
# of the trending and volume feeds: they are all rankings of tokens that are
# ALREADY established, so their membership barely changes.
#
# The newest-pools feed has the opposite problem. Solana creates roughly
# three pools per second, so one 20-row page spans about seven seconds and
# ~500 pools are born between two refreshes. It is a firehose, and the
# pipeline manages only ~190 evaluations an hour.
#
# Neither the volume nor the age of that feed can be used directly:
#
#   Volume -- more mints than the pipeline can evaluate is not more data. The
#   scarce resource is evaluation slots, not candidates. What a wider draw
#   buys is INDEPENDENCE between observations, not throughput.
#
#   Age -- a pool seconds old has no price at any provider yet, so
#   record_candidate() drops it at its `price <= 0` guard and the slot
#   produces nothing at all. Worse, one that does get written can never be
#   marked and ages into ABANDONED three hours later.
#
# So capture is decoupled from evaluation. New pools are filed in an
# in-memory pen with their creation time, wait until they are old enough to
# be priced, and only then join the candidate list -- for a bounded window,
# because a mint the sampler never happened to draw should make room for a
# fresher one rather than sit there forever.

# Pages of the newest-pools feed per refresh.
#
# The first version of this note assumed ~3 pools/second and described the
# feed as a firehose. probe_new_pools.py measured 0.67/s: only ~120 pools are
# born between refreshes, and six pages already see ~69% of them. So this is
# far closer to an enumeration than it looked, the marginal page is worth
# little, and page 7 is where the probe took a 429.
#
# The launch rate is not a constant. Re-run probe_new_pools.py before raising
# this -- at a busier hour the same 4 pages cover proportionally less.
DISCOVERY_NEW_POOL_PAGES=4

# How long a captured pool waits before it may be evaluated. Long enough for
# DexScreener to have indexed it and for a first candle to exist.
DISCOVERY_NEW_POOL_MIN_AGE_MINUTES=15

# Minimum pool reserve, checked once at capture, when it is effectively the
# launch liquidity.
#
# This is a SAMPLING FRAME, not a gate, and the distinction matters. It does
# not duplicate B_SENTINEL: that gate wants >= $20k of tradeable depth, four
# times this floor, so pool-sourced tokens still arrive thin and are still
# REJECTED for depth. The control group keeps its thin tokens and "rejected"
# keeps meaning "rejected for any reason". What this does is decide which
# launches are in the experiment at all -- the same job "trending" does for
# the other sources. Without a frame the source is unbounded: the vast
# majority of new pools launch with a few hundred dollars and are abandoned
# within the hour, and they would crowd out the sample rather than enlarge it.
#
# Known bias, accepted: a pool that launches under this floor and grows past
# it is excluded here. It is not lost, only slower -- once it has real volume
# DISCOVERY_POOL_PAGES picks it up on its own.
#
# WHY 25,000 AND NOT 5,000
# It was 5,000 for about half an hour, picked on an assumed 25% pass rate.
# The probe measured 12%, and measured the shape: 85% of new pools clear
# $1,000, 27% clear $2,500, 12% clear $5,000, with the mass in a tight band
# near $2.2k -- a launchpad seeding pools with a standard amount, not a
# natural distribution.
#
# Sampling that band files hundreds of tokens an hour whose verdict is a
# foregone conclusion, into a control arm already ~5x the approved arm. The
# standard error on the cohort comparison is dominated by APPROVED, so a
# 99th rejected token buys almost nothing while consuming one of only ~190
# evaluation slots an hour.
#
# MIND THE UNITS. This value is a pool's reserve -- TVL, both sides.
# B_SENTINEL judges tradeable depth, which is min(TVL / 2, reported
# one-sided), so its $20,000 bar sits near $40,000 of RESERVE. $25,000 of
# reserve is at most ~$12,500 of depth: nothing captured here clears the
# gate at the moment of capture. What decides approval is whether the pool
# grows during the 15-minute hold, which is precisely the uncertainty worth
# sampling -- both verdicts stay reachable for every token filed.
#
# Do NOT raise this to $40,000 or beyond. At or above the gate's own level
# approval becomes a tautology: the sampler would offer only tokens that
# already pass the gate under test, and this source's REJECTED arm -- along
# with any ability to compare the two -- disappears. Both ends of the usable
# range are pinned by tests/test_discovery.py [PEN/FRAME].
DISCOVERY_NEW_POOL_MIN_RESERVE_USD=25000

# How long a promoted mint stays in the candidate list. The pipeline draws
# uniformly at random at ~190 evaluations/hour, so a mint needs to sit in the
# list a while to have a fair chance of being drawn at all. Too short and
# most promotions expire unevaluated; too long and the list fills with
# tokens already recorded, which the re-entry cooldown refuses anyway.
DISCOVERY_NEW_POOL_RETAIN_MINUTES=45

# Ceiling on promotions offered per refresh, so one unusually busy stretch
# cannot crowd the trending sources out of DISCOVERY_MAX_CANDIDATES.
DISCOVERY_NEW_POOL_MAX_ACTIVE=150

# Bound on pen size. Entries expire on their own; this only defends against
# unbounded growth if the promotion path ever stops draining it.
DISCOVERY_NEW_POOL_PEN_MAX=4000

# Header credential for the RPC above (Triton/rpcpool x-token). Leave blank to
# use the keyless public endpoint, which is rate-limited and not suitable for
# sustained use -- chain-computed holder concentration alone needs ~4 calls per
# evaluated token.
SOLANA_RPC_X_TOKEN=
